  "configuration_id": "config_014",
  "reason": "Evidence: In RNA-only multiprofile, scvi_n200 (0.650) substantially outperforms scgpt (0.530), a 0.120 gap. In the multimodal multiprofile setting, our best config (config_027: scvi_n200 + imagenet_vit_l, transformer, weighted) achieves 0.725. However, we have no data on whether the RNA encoder choice still matters when a strong image encoder is present. Question: Does pairing scgpt with the same image encoder, aggregation, and fusion (imagenet_vit_l, transformer, weighted) close the gap to scvi_n200, or does RNA encoder quality remain critical even in multimodal settings? This is a clean single-variable comparison (scgpt vs scvi_n200) with all other factors held at their best-known values. Why best next experiment: Under limited budget, resolving whether the RNA encoder is a dominant or secondary design axis in the multimodal regime directly informs whether further search should focus on RNA encoder variants or on other axes (aggregation, fusion, image encoder). If scgpt+image ≈ scvi_n200+image, it frees the search to explore other dimensions; if the gap persists, it confirms scvi_n200 as the necessary backbone and justifies concentrating remaining budget on tuning around it."
}

